    {
      "field_name": "diagnostic_criteria",
      "value": "symptoms consistent with SMS; painful muscle spasms secondary to SMS",
      "quote": "The patient is a 34-year-old woman who has had symptoms consistent with SMS for more than 12 years but was diagnosed only recently (approximately 1 year ago) with it. The patient was treated with plasmapheresis and cyclophosphamide. She had two courses of plasmapheresis with symptomatic relief of her muscle spasms for approximately 2 months. Each course consisted of approximately six exchanges. The patient was referred from the neurology outpatient unit because of her GI complaints. Her main complaint was constipation. She had some upper GI symptoms that included nausea, early satiety, and mild reflux symptoms. In addition, she had pharyngeal dysphagia, an intermittent hold-up of food in the pharynx. Abdominal films showed no evidence of bowel dilation. The results of an anorectal motility study were normal-negative for sphincteric function. A gastric-emptying study using an isotope-labeled solid meal (chicken liver and beef stew) showed delayed gastric emptying. The time it took to empty 50% of the meal (T%) was 221 minutes. The percentage of solids retained at 120 minutes was 76% (normal values for T % and percent of retention at 2 hours are respectively, <150 minutes and <70%). An upper GI endoscopy and esophageal motility study were not performed because of her painful muscle spasms secondary to SMS.",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
